  - \paragraph{The implementation.} \code{collect\_eq\_rows} records, per block written, the cells, the kept column positions and the bases; \code{lpi\_eq\_dopvar} records the row range of the call when the program carries the field \code{sopeq} (the executives set \code{prog.sopeq = \{\}} after \code{lpiprogram}; no other program gains a field); \code{lpigetdual\_sop} places \code{prog.solinfo.y} at the recorded positions and returns $\mcl Y$ as an \code{opvar}. Two solver facts were checked by running (10/08/2026, 1-D $H_\infty$ gain executive, 169 rows): \code{sossolve} divides $b$ by its norm before the solve, and the dual of the scaled problem has the same maximizer as the dual of the original, so $y$ needs no rescaling; and with \code{sos\_opts.simplify} on, \code{sossolve} removes dependent rows and restores the multipliers into the original coordinates with zeros at the removed rows. In both settings $b^Ty$ equals the primal objective to $5\times10^{-9}$.
