C:/Users/mpeet/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/363c499c6332a503/874c85cb-c18d-4876-bea3-39cdd053e21f/scratchpad/sop/tex/sopvar_duality_pruning_notes.tex:430:
  - \paragraph{A target linear in a free operator.} The support rule bounds a fixed target. The block $\mcl A^*\mcl Q+\mcl Q^*\mcl A$ of a Q form is composed \emph{before} the equality $\mcl T^*\mcl Q=\mcl P_{\mrm s}$ is imposed, so its support is that of the whole \code{lpivar} family of $\mcl Q$ under composition, a lower kernel to degree $9$ with $D_{\min}=4$ on the plant \code{io1}, and not that of the optimal $\mcl Q$; the reader then sizes the slack at $(D,w)=(5,3)$ where $(2,2)$ is the measured minimum (Table~\ref{tab:kyp}). The option \code{like} of \code{lpi\_ineq\_sop} reads the support of the fixed positive operator of the LPI, the storage, and lays the degrees over the spaces of the target; on the storage the rule gives $(2,2)$ at the stock light level and $(2,3)$ at heavy. The executives use \code{like} with the storage for every slack and raise the rule by $dw_Q=1$ in the Q forms and $dw_P=2$ in the coercive forms, the smallest values that reached the numerical value on one 1-D and one 2-D plant.
  + \paragraph{A target linear in a free operator.} The support rule bounds a fixed target. The block $\mcl A^*\mcl Q+\mcl Q^*\mcl A$ of a Q form is composed \emph{before} the equality $\mcl T^*\mcl Q=\mcl P_{\mrm s}$ is imposed, so its support is that of the whole \code{lpivar} family of $\mcl Q$ under composition, a lower kernel to degree $9$ with $D_{\min}=4$ on the plant \code{io1}, and not that of the optimal $\mcl Q$; the reader then sizes the slack at $(D,w)=(5,3)$ where $(2,2)$ is the measured minimum (Table~\ref{tab:kyp}). The option \code{like} of \code{lpi\_ineq} reads the support of the fixed positive operator of the LPI, the storage, and lays the degrees over the spaces of the target; on the storage the rule gives $(2,2)$ at the stock light level and $(2,3)$ at heavy. The executives use \code{like} with the storage for every slack and raise the rule by $dw_Q=1$ in the Q forms and $dw_P=2$ in the coercive forms, the smallest values that reached the numerical value on one 1-D and one 2-D plant.
C:/Users/mpeet/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/363c499c6332a503/874c85cb-c18d-4876-bea3-39cdd053e21f/scratchpad/sop/tex/sopvar_duality_pruning_notes.tex:466:
  - \paragraph{What the code does.} \code{eq\_opts\_sopvar} measures the reach of the diagonal term of a block with multi-index $\alpha$ by declaring one block at a time, $\mrm{reach}(\alpha)=\prod_dR_d$ with $R_d=\{1\}$ if $\alpha_d=1$ and $\{2,3\}$ otherwise, since adjoining turns a lower integral into an upper one; it drops a block only when the target is zero on all of $\mrm{reach}(\alpha)$, and marks a direction separable when the lower and upper cells of the target agree there. \code{lpi\_ineq\_sop} applies the rule to the diagonal block of each $L_2$ space of the target (option \code{prune}, on by default), passes the survivors as the \code{include} of the lift, and expands the separable full-integral index into the lower and upper indices, a superset of the unseparated reading. Soundness is immediate, since dropping basis operators shrinks the cone; losslessness is Lemma~\ref{lem:deltacell} for the multiplier cell, and for the integral cells, which several blocks reach, it is argued in the code header from the measured reach sets and not proved here. The stock 2-D rule (\code{get\_eq\_opts\_2D}) drops a component whenever its own cell alone is zero; Proposition~\ref{prop:zerodiag} does not imply that, since the diagonal of a lower block also reaches the upper and mixed cells, and it can lose a certificate. A per-monomial version of the argument is false (measured 09/21/2026): the map from Gram entries to output monomials is many-to-one, $256$ entries onto $10$ output degrees at $\mrm{int}=\mrm{mult}=3$, and no entry is isolated at a position.
  + \paragraph{What the code does.} \code{get\_eq\_opts} measures the reach of the diagonal term of a block with multi-index $\alpha$ by declaring one block at a time, $\mrm{reach}(\alpha)=\prod_dR_d$ with $R_d=\{1\}$ if $\alpha_d=1$ and $\{2,3\}$ otherwise, since adjoining turns a lower integral into an upper one; it drops a block only when the target is zero on all of $\mrm{reach}(\alpha)$, and marks a direction separable when the lower and upper cells of the target agree there. \code{lpi\_ineq} applies the rule to the diagonal block of each $L_2$ space of the target (option \code{prune}, on by default), passes the survivors as the \code{include} of the lift, and expands the separable full-integral index into the lower and upper indices, a superset of the unseparated reading. Soundness is immediate, since dropping basis operators shrinks the cone; losslessness is Lemma~\ref{lem:deltacell} for the multiplier cell, and for the integral cells, which several blocks reach, it is argued in the code header from the measured reach sets and not proved here. The stock 2-D rule (\code{get\_eq\_opts\_2D}) drops a component whenever its own cell alone is zero; Proposition~\ref{prop:zerodiag} does not imply that, since the diagonal of a lower block also reaches the upper and mixed cells, and it can lose a certificate. A per-monomial version of the argument is false (measured 09/21/2026): the map from Gram entries to output monomials is many-to-one, $256$ entries onto $10$ output degrees at $\mrm{int}=\mrm{mult}=3$, and no entry is isolated at a position.
